t he  was a very demanding father also a very  loving father but all that he demanded  of his children was that they cultivate  their own particular talents and  interests to the highest possible degree  um and I think in terms of Nine Hostages  to fortune that's one of the reasons  obviously that I chose the name for the  book and he was somebody Joe Kennedy was  somebody who toured an extraordinary  degree  um controlled his own destiny but  couldn't obviously control the fact that  some of the most important and most  precious things in his life his children  particularly young Joe Kennedy and  Kathleen were taken from him in ways  that were absolutely beyond his control  and in terms of I chose this is the  title also because it relates to his  reasons for supporting Franklin  Roosevelt originally in 1932 um not long  after the beginning of the campaign he  said to a reporter um you know basically  my children the most important thing in  the world to me and I feel that um some  modicum of socialism during the Great  Depression and this from a very  conservative capitalist I was the only  thing that would help avoid revolution  in this country and safeguard the the  fortune that he had masked to allow his  children to go on and hopefully  participate in civic life of the country  that had made their advantages possible  so he supported Franklin Roosevelt  preventatively if you will yeah and with  a certain hesitation to him in his  relationship with Roosevelt began on a  very bad note and when Roosevelt during  the First World War when rose wanted out  of the book that rose well actually made  him cry yeah and I think that that the  beginning of their relationship that  began under such hostile terms when  Roosevelt was the Under Secretary of the  Navy and Joe Kennedy was the manager of  a wartime shipyard rose  ordered armed Marines to come and remove  a ship from the Quincy shipyard where he  worked before the Argentine government  had paid for the ships and at that point  my grandfather said Roosevelt was the  toughest negotiator I'd ever come up  against and I broke down on my knees on  the dock that day and began to cry and  over the course of the 30s they they  came to know each other once again when  Roosevelt became a candidate and Joe  Kennedy was brought into the Roosevelt  camp as a very unusual figure I mean he  was one of the few businessmen a few  staunch conservative capitalists who  became part of the Roosevelt camp um but  it was through that political alliance  that they began to sort of have a an  appreciation for one another's talents  and abilities although it's certainly  clear from Joe Kennedy's letters that he  always had reservations about Roosevelt  and was very concerned about who else  might be getting to Roosevelt and  feeding him advice and and the people  around Roosevelt were just as concerned  at least about Joe Kennedy getting to  Roseman yeah absolutely and so he he  took up the jobs at the Securities and  Exchange Commission and at the Maritime  Commission over the course of the 30s um  with a little bit of resentment I mean  certainly they were wonderful posts and  he by most accounts at the time  acquitted himself very admirably but  they weren't really what he had hoped  for him and he throughout his life had  wanted to be the secretary of the  Treasury and his wouldn't say his  nemesis but certainly one of the people  he liked least in the administration was  Henry Morgenthau and who beat him to it  in effect so when the posting came along  in London um he jumped at it and went  and Roosevelt's account of why send an  Irishman to London at a time like that  when war was looming was while it's the  greatest joke in the world with typical  Roosevelt influencing and I'm similar to  when Roosevelt appointed him to head the  Securities and Exchange Commission he  said in effect it takes a thief to catch  a thief yeah  the Fox to guard the chicken coop and  and so Joe Kennedy went to London  believing I'm rightly or wrongly that  Roosevelt had given him a mandate of  absolute US neutrality in the case of  any European war and there's some  question as to whether Joe Kennedy was  reading his own viewpoint into  Roosevelt's parting advice to him but it  was really that issue that broke their  relationship apart and by the time he  gave this address where he described his  children his nine hostages to fortune in  support of Roosevelt's third term  election it really was a defining moment  in the relationship it was the last  thing that Joe Kennedy did on  Roosevelt's behalf he was very angry  very grudging at the time that he made  this speech because he felt that his  power and authority in London had been  subverted and that most of for example  in destroyers for bases negotiations  that had gone on over the course of the  previous year had been carried out  largely through the British Embassy in  Washington rather than through the  American Embassy in London and he knew  for example that Roosevelt and Churchill  were corresponding secretly as we all  know now but he only knew that because  he was to use his own phrase a sort of  glorified errand boy delivering the  messages back and forth between  Churchill and the American Embassy  Amanda what was his real position in  London philosophically he's been accused  of being an anti-semite he's accused of  being an appeaser  he said that Chamberlain and and Pope  Pius the 12th were among his greatest  heroes of course he said that then  without the benefit of hindsight and we  have to take it in that context where  did he really stand peace at any price  keep America out of war no matter what  that was his own philosophy in World War  two did he carried all the way through  in World War one rather did he carry  that all the way through in World War  two also well his own sort of pithy  slogan as to what his viewpoint was in  World War two was all aid to Britain  short of war which is um which is  something that he arrived at over the  course of his ambassadorship he began as  ambassadorship  as a very close friend and a great  admirer of Neville Chamberlain and also  many members of Chamberlain's cabinet  particularly Lord Halifax the foreign  minister at the time and Sir John Simon  who is the Chancellor of Exchequer and  at first that closeness to the cabinet  to the prime minister himself was  actually a very useful thing in  Washington because Kennedy's dispatches  were um you know to quote high-ranking  members of the State Department at the  time the most penetrating that had been  received in years um and actually one  thing that was fascinating to me was  that in press accounts from 1938 and  early 1939 the one thing that he's most  often accused of by members of the press  and politicians across the spectrum in  America was that he was too pro British  to actually carry out his duties  correctly in in the UK and it was  believed that he wouldn't be able to  represent American interest because he  was too taken in by the British and he  came around to believing eventually that  Hitler wouldn't stop with Europe and  actually was the proponent of sending  aid to Britain insofar as we would be  able to spare any during the destroyers  for bases negotiations for example um  but beyond that he really didn't feel  that we should enter the war unless  attacked and he did actually believe  that we'd be attacked but thought that  if the attack would probably come after  the conquest of Europe and and he felt  that the Germans would probably or the  axis in general would probably look  towards the Americas and that the attack  would basically come through South  America and we'd go to war over the  Monroe Doctrine and that assisting  Britain was nothing more than an attempt  to buy ourselves time during our own  rearmament effort and his relationship  with Churchill I think is really  fascinating in this light because um he  was very offended by Churchill's  beautifu