till me from an  English major right  oh yeah um but I think on a more  personal scale a minute  I've never really understood what it is  that makes people so interested but I  can only assume from what people come up  and tell me um particulate events like  this that very often people will say you  know I got such strength from your  grandmother because my own children died  very young or something or um you know I  come from a big family myself and I know  what it means to be a child from a big  family and try and you know get some  attention or make something of yourself  among a big crew of people um and I  found that people seem to relate to them  in an incredibly personal way or even  you know with with my grandfather people  often say to me no I had a very  difficult father myself and um you know  I'm really fascinated with how children  deal with very you know larger-than-life  um  paternal or maternal figures so I can  only assume that people do see something  themselves in them there's sort of an  ambivalence that runs through the book  at least with the introduction it is  sometimes them and it's sometimes us it  sometimes Joe Kennedy and it's sometimes  grandpa which is it for you  I think it's definitely both I mean in  the introduction I tried to use both  because he's both a historical figure Oh  Joe Kennedy and he's also grandpa um and  I think that one of the things  especially in my first memory of him  going to see him and have him discover  that everybody has a different name for  him that that really stayed with me for  the rest of my life and I never quite  could figure out how this person was  that many different things at once I  mean he was when I first learned about  him he was just grandpa and he was the  adored father and grandfather figure  around the house and then as I got older  it began to occur to me that other  people seem to know about him too and as  I went into school I began reading about  him and found out that you know not on