esident had polio and I think  he's speaking of an America that um you  know in part is a very different America  because of the very media technologies  that he um tried to cultivate and make  use of to his own ends over the course  of his life and technologies that  posthumously or even during his lifetime  came back to bite him in the sense that  we know a great deal more about him than  we might have had they not existed and  we know a great deal more about our  presidents and feel our own politicians  in general and feel probably greater  right now to have access to their  personal dealings  but I'm not sure he even envisioned a  Watergate or sometimes I'm sort of  watershed event that brought that about  and I think just you know wanted to  create an image of America that was and  and wanted in America that was as good  as it could be  would he have survived the kind of  scrutiny we give public figures today  well it's interesting you call him a  public figure because I think in some  ways he was had one foot in public life  in one foot in private life I mean  certainly after the falling out with  Roosevelt he retreated into the  background a great deal as you point out  the book did not even plan to publish  his memoirs his diplomatic memoirs for  fear or concerned that it might  embarrass his kids or affect their  political ambitions and she said let  them carry the ball you know it's  interesting about that I mean that's  very often said but one thing that I  found about his um his reluctance to  publish his memoir about his time in  Britain which began as a kind of you  know celebration of the fact that he was  the first Irish American ambassador to  go to London and very quickly as he  began to fall out with Roosevelt and  their disagreement began to drive a  wedge between them over the issue of  neutrality um he began to sort of  document very carefully all of the  slights and insults um and all of the  what he felt were untrue that Roosevelt  was puttin